WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.630
Bill Gates: Good Morning Hank! It's Tuesday!

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:05.420
John Green: Woke up very early in Addis Ababa
a few weeks ago and began a long drive South.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.740
Slums at the edge of the city gave way to
beautiful vistas and then the road worsened

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.089
and I became a bobblehead until at last, we
reached a rural healthcare center.

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:17.050
So Hank, here's a story I heard a lot in Ethiopia.
Traditionally, Ethiopian women haven't named

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.240
their children for several months after they
were born because the risk of losing the babies

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.269
was just too high. But lately, thanks to the
astonishing drop in mortality rates due to

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:29.910
smart aid and good government investment,
Ethiopian women have begun to name their babies

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:30.539
at birth.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:33.950
It's a great story and it's one that tugs
at the heartstrings and makes you want to

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.680
open your wallet. It's also - well, the truth
turns out to be maybe less narratively interesting,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.469
but, in a way, more encouraging.

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:43.520
So the Ethiopian health care system is organized
into tiers. You begin with hospitals, but

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.270
with more than 80% of people living in rural
areas, the next level, the healthcare center

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:52.010
is vital . Here there are lots of medications
and vaccines, there's a lab technician who

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.840
can diagnose parasites and operate this centrifuge.
Some healthcare centers even perform C-sections.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.379
All this despite the fact that almost none
of them have running water. When I asked center

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:03.510
manager, Abd Al-Kadir what he needed most he
answered immediately - water.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.840
While we were there, I also met Bill Gates,
the founder of Microsoft and Sue Desmond-Hellman,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.250
the CEO of the Gates Foundation. To be honest
with you Hank, I kind of assumed that Bill

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.640
and Sue were visiting Ethiopia for like, a
photo op. but yeah, no, they were there to

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.500
ask questions and lots of them - to ask how
the lab tech typed malaria without a particular

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.790
type of microscope, which vaccines were stored
in what quantities, how many workers were

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.530
needed to run the center efficiently and so
on...

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.280
And watching Bill and Sue gravitate away from
cameras and toward health workers, it became

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.310
clear to me that the Gates Foundation's fundamental
principle, that all human lives have equal

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.740
value isn't just rhetoric.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.420
So then I travelled to the next healthcare
tier, the outpost where health extension workers

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.600
like Utagazu, with a tenth grade education
and a year of training, provide basic care

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.619
in rural areas. Utagazu showed me her well-organized
medical records, the delivery room in which

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:50.719
many babies are born and wall charts measuring
vaccination rates.

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:54.110
But mostly, she and her coworkers were busy
taking care of people, like this little boy

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.659
was being tested for malaria with a pin-prick
test that has dramatically improved diagnosis

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:02.299
and treatment. When he was found to be positive,
the health extension workers could begin treatment

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:03.009
immediately.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:07.579
This little guy was extremely sick with diarrhea
that was suspected to be a virus. Diarrhea

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:10.980
remains one of the leading causes of child
death in Ethiopia, even though it is always

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.140
almost survivable if kids can stay hydrated,
so this boy went home with a rehydration solution

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.910
that might well have saved his life.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.990
And there is one last tier of the Ethiopian
health care system, the women's health army.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:26.750
My favorite army of all time! These are volunteers
who take maternal and infant care into their

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.730
communities, talking about everything from
family planning to how to get folic acid.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.410
A member of the health army invited me into
her home and showed me the map that she uses

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:39.020
to keep track of who's sick, who's pregnant
and who has a small child, and this is how,

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:44.850
with limited resources, information and care
travels the last few miles into rural communities.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:49.110
Hank, I don't wanna minimize the amazing work
that the Gates Foundation and other organizations

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.600
are doing in Ethiopia, or the importance of
technologies like the easy to read malaria

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.410
tests or this amazing thermos that keeps vaccines
cold for 50 days without electricity.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:03.250
But the real heroes of Ethiopia's health successes
are Ethiopian. The women volunteering in their

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:07.950
communities, health extension workers treating
disease and compiling data, mothers fighting

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.410
to keep their kids healthy despite the immense
challenges of poverty. Their progress has

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:17.910
been real and should be celebrated, but there's
also much to do. Kids should not die of diarrhea

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.990
or pneumonia or malaria, and far too many
still do.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:25.250
So, about that naming at birth thing. I asked
these women about it and they all agreed that

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.590
kids do get named at birth now which never
used to happen. Some said that it might be

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:34.320
because child mortality is lower these days,
but most felt that it was because now they

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.350
have to fill out birth certificates. That
doesn't tug on the heartstrings in quite the

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:43.490
same way, but in the end, it's maybe an even
better sign because good health records are

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.420
vital to good healthcare.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.370
In short Hank, when it comes to healthcare,
heart warming narratives and boring bureaucracy

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.010
are not always mutually exclusive. Anyway,
then I rode in a helicopter with Bill Gates

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.630
which was terrifying and hilarious, and I
will definitely tell you about it next week

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.470
unless I get meningitis again or something...

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.200
Hank, DFTBA, I will see you on Friday!

